  <!-- Section 1: The Physics -->
  <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-black text-gray-950 mt-12 mb-4 tracking-tight">
    1. The Thermodynamic Reality: Why Cold Weather Increases EV Energy Consumption
  </h2>
  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    To understand why winter increases electric car operational costs, it helps to dismantle a persistent automotive myth: <em>the electricity grid does not charge you more per unit of power when it snows</em>. Rather, an EV consumes significantly more kilowatt-hours from the wall to propel itself the same distance.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-4 leading-relaxed">
    Internal combustion engine (ICE) vehicles are notoriously thermally inefficient, converting only 25% to 30% of the energy stored in gasoline into forward momentum. The remaining 70% to 75% is rejected as waste heat through the radiator and exhaust pipe. In winter, an ICE vehicle simply opens a heater core valve to divert that abundant waste heat into the passenger cabin at zero extra fuel penalty.
  </p>

  <p class="mb-6 leading-relaxed">
    In sharp contrast, an electric vehicle powertrain operates at 88% to 92% electrical efficiency, generating negligible waste heat. When outside temperatures drop to 20°F (-7°C) or lower, four thermodynamic factors conspire to increase your energy consumption:
  </p>

  <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8 not-prose">
    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
        <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-md bg-red-100 text-red-700 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-fire"></i>
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950">Cabin Climate Control Draw</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
        Forced-air resistive cabin heaters draw <strong>3.0 kW to 6.0 kW continuously</strong>. If you drive at 35 mph in stop-and-go suburban traffic for one hour, the heater alone burns 3 to 6 kWh—representing a 20% to 30% energy penalty before wheels even turn. Heat pump systems (found in the <a href="/blog/ev-charging-cost-us-2026" class="text-emerald-700 font-bold hover:underline">Tesla Model Y</a> and modern crossovers) are 2x to 3x more efficient, but still require 1.0 to 2.5 kW in freezing conditions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
        <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-md bg-blue-100 text-blue-700 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-atom"></i>
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950">Electrolyte Sluggishness &amp; BMS Warming</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
        Lithium-ion liquid electrolytes become viscous below 32°F (0°C). Internal resistance spikes, slowing lithium ion transfer between cathode and anode. To protect cells from destructive lithium plating, the Battery Management System (BMS) activates internal heating loops, consuming <strong>2.0 to 4.0 kWh of pack energy</strong> to warm the battery into its sweet spot (60°F–77°F / 15°C–25°C).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 mb-2">
        <span class="w-6 h-6 rounded-md bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 flex items-center justify-center text-xs font-black">
          <i class="fa-solid fa-plug-circle-bolt"></i>
        </span>
        <h3 class="text-sm font-black text-gray-950">Elevated Level 2 Charging Losses</h3>
      </div>
      <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-2">
        During moderate summer weather, AC-to-DC onboard charging inverter losses hover between <strong>8% and 10%</strong>. In freezing ambient weather, losses climb to <strong>12% to 15%</strong> because the car must spend the first 30 to 45 minutes of a Level 2 session diverting grid electricity to heat the battery pack before it can absorb high current safely.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="p-5 bg-white border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-2xl shadow-xs">
